     "type": "text",
     "text": "{\"count\": 2, \"rationale\": \"Deploying mfa:admin and mfa:dbadmin together blocks exfiltration. Phishing is always available, so the attacker always gets foothold_workstation and stolen_user. They can reach the internal network, get onto the app and CI servers, kerberoast for stolen_admin, and leak stolen_dbadmin from the CI server. However, every route to the db, fileserver or backup (auth_db, auth_fileserver, auth_backup) needs either NOT mfa_dbadmin or admin_dc/admin_adfs. Those admin rights need a foothold on the DC or ADFS, and auth_dc and auth_adfs both need NOT mfa_admin. So with both MFA controls deployed, no exfiltration target is reachable. No single control is enough: (1) with only segmented, phishing plus escalate_workstation gives admin_workstation, so the attacker still pivots; (2) with only mfa:admin, CI-server secrets give stolen_dbadmin and auth_db succeeds; (3) with only mfa:dbadmin, stolen_admin leads to auth_dc, admin_dc, and then the db; (4) with only mfa:user, the phished workstation foothold bypasses it; (5) no single patch closes every path. Therefore the minimum is 2.\"}"
    }
   ],
